<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Salerno</h1><p class="st-meta">849 CE – 1077 CE · capital Salerno</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Lombard principality of southern Italy, home of the famous medical school of Salerno, conquered by Robert Guiscard in 1077.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>A long civil war in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-benevento">Principality of Benevento</a> ended in 849 with a formal partition: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siconulf">Siconulf</a> became prince of Salerno, ruling the western half including the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radelchis-i-of-benevento">Radelchis</a> kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Salerno was a prosperous coastal city with links to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-amalfi">Amalfi</a> and the Byzantine world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schola-medica-salernitana">Schola Medica Salernitana</a>, the first great medical school of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, flourished there from the 10th century, drawing on Greek, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Arabic learning. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guaimar-iv-of-salerno">Guaimar IV</a> (1027–1052) the principality reached its height, controlling Amalfi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorrento">Sorrento</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-capua">Capua</a>, and he invested the Norman leaders with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guaimar-iv-of-salerno">Guaimar IV</a> was murdered in 1052, Norman power grew rapidly. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a>, who had married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichelgaita">Sichelgaita</a>, sister of Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gisulf-ii-of-salerno">Gisulf II</a>, besieged Salerno and took it in 1077, making it his capital. The principality became part of the Norman <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-apulia-and-calabria">Duchy of Apulia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-salerno.jpg" alt="Principality of Salerno" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: OttavianoLolnoob · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Principality_of_Salerno_(871_A.D.).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>849 CE – 1077 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Salerno</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Siconulf, Guaimar IV, Gisulf II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Italy</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Salerno</dd></dl></aside></div>
